Chapter 30 — Part 1
Open mic night had the floor warmer than usual. A small stage was cleared near the back, lights low, a couple of early acts already signed up on the board. Janice and Tatsuya were at the bar when Curtis came through the side door in the work uniform. The lip was clean. No new marks. They both smiled.
“Beardy,” Janice said. “Given you don’t look like you fought a train, I suspect you took it easy?”
Curtis shrugged, tying the apron. “Yeah. Something like that.”
Hina stepped up beside him, already clocked in, and smiled at Janice. “He renovated his suite. Drywall, flooring, the whole thing. Had workers in. Wore weighted cuffs all day instead of going to the forest. Eight hundred pounds. Then he came home and showered like a normal person.”
Tatsuya’s brows rose. Janice looked between them, the smile turning into something closer to approval. “Renovating and weighted walking. That’s the kind of ‘taking it easy’ I can live with.” She nodded toward the floor. “You’re back on tables tonight. Try not to ruin it. And if anyone asks about the open mic, you’re welcome to put your name down. No pressure.”
Curtis glanced at the stage, then at Hina. “We’ll see.”
He headed for his section. Hina bumped his arm on the way past, warm. The club lights stayed soft. The low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet while open mic night got underway and, for once, he walked onto the floor without a fresh cut for anyone to worry over.
Curtis watched two acts finish before he put his name on the board. Loyalty had settled in slow since Japan. Janice had given him the job when he had nothing local, then kept giving him shifts even when he walked in looking like he’d fought the concrete with his face. That earned the stage more than any open-mic urge.
He picked an upbeat track the house system already had. When his turn hit he stepped up, adjusted the mic, and started.
The voice that came out was clean and full, nothing like the low humming at the bar. It carried. Patrons who had been talking turned. A few couples moved to the small open floor without being asked. The beat stayed bright. He sang through the lines about stepping to the rhythm, about not needing words, about dancing while the night held. His tone stayed easy. The room leaned in anyway.
Free Love moved with the sound. It wasn’t just the voice. The passive pull thickened under the lights, warm and insistent. Cheeks flushed deeper across the tables. A woman near the front swayed harder than the music required. Hina, working the edge of the floor, felt it hit and had to focus on her tray. Even Janice, arms crossed near the bar, watched with a look that was half approval and half wary of how many drinks were suddenly being ordered.
Curtis finished the last chorus, let the instrumental ride, and stepped back with a short nod. Applause came quick and genuine. A few people were still dancing as the next name was called. He handed the mic off and headed for his section, the low, steady pull in his chest quiet again, while the enhanced warmth he’d left on the floor took another minute to fade and the club kept moving around the space he’d just filled.
Curtis stepped off the small stage as the house lights eased back to their usual amber. Applause followed him—warmer than the first time he’d sung here, more people already on their feet from the dancing. A couple of regulars clapped him on the shoulder on the way past. One of the women from his earlier tables smiled wide enough to show it and immediately flagged Tatsuya to ask if the host took requests now. Curtis nodded at them, easy, and headed for the bar to grab water.
Janice met him there, tablet under her arm, the wary edge from the Free Love spike already settling into approval. “Better than last time. Room actually moved. You write that one too?”
“Nah. Found it. Figured upbeat fit open mic better than whatever I usually hum.” He took the water Tatsuya slid him. “Thanks for the slot.”
“You earned the repeat.” She tapped the screen. “Weekend ones stay open if you want them. Customers are already asking. Just keep the face clean between now and then.”
Tatsuya’s mouth curved. “Told you the voice would work. And love, you’re definitely not firing him.”
“Not tonight,” Janice said. The smile stayed.
Hina appeared at the end of the counter with a tray balanced, cheeks flushed deeper than the club lights explained. She waited until Janice and Tatsuya turned back to a waving table before she spoke, quieter. “That was really good. They were dancing. I didn’t know you could… pull a room like that.”
Curtis loosened his tie the rest of the way, expression even. “Just a song. You sticking around till close?”
“Yeah. Flyers for the late crowd.” She shifted, smiling small. “If you’re walking after, I still don’t mind the company.”
“Works for me.”
The floor noise rose again around them—new drinks, low music under the next act, the ordinary rhythm of the shift. A few patrons were still swaying near the stage. Free Love’s thicker pull had started to fade, but the warmth lingered in flushed faces and extra orders. Curtis stayed at the bar a moment longer, water in hand, while Hina lingered closer than the tray required and the low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet under the lights he’d just stepped out of.
Somewhere across the city a phone would eventually catch a clip. The group chat would light up again. For now the club kept moving, and he went back to his section with the applause still settling behind him.
