The next act had just started when the side door banged open. An angry man in a rumpled dress shirt shoved past the distracted security and zeroed in on Curtis’s section. He was across the floor before anyone fully registered him.
“You son of a bitch!”
He lunged. Curtis met the grab, caught the wrist, and turned the momentum into a spin. The man stumbled. Curtis guided him through a half-turn like they were mid-dance, then dipped and set him firmly into the open chair at the nearest table. The motion was smooth enough that a few patrons laughed, thinking it was part of the night’s entertainment.
Curtis didn’t stop. Air pockets formed under his boots. He glided a short circle around the table, snagged a bottle and two glasses from a passing tray with one hand, and dropped into the opposite seat. The crowd, still riding the earlier song, clapped like it was a bit. Someone whistled.
He poured both glasses, slid one across, and kept his voice low. “Keep your voice down. What’s your problem, man?”
The man was breathing hard, face red, but the public seating had knocked the immediate swing out of him. “You. You’re the new guy pulling every regular from our place. Club got shut down last month. Half my clients followed the noise here. You’re racking the tables I used to have.”
Curtis frowned. He glanced at the man’s clothes—decent cut, just angry—and the way a couple of nearby women were already watching with interest. “Instead of attacking me, come jump ship. Janice is really nice. You’d probably have to work your way back up, but I’m sure you’d do well. You’re not half bad looking.”
A woman approached the table, eyes bright. “Shiyu?! Oh my god, do you work here? Can you come sit with me?”
Curtis smiled at her, easy. “We’re currently having a meeting. Maybe later, miss.”
She smiled back, flushed, and nodded before drifting off. Shiyu stared at Curtis, then at the glass, the fight draining into confusion.
Janice and Tatsuya arrived together. Janice’s smile was professional and sharp. Tatsuya’s hand rested near his earpiece. They took in the seated man, the poured drinks, and the crowd that had already decided it was entertainment.
“Everything alright here?” Janice asked.
Curtis nodded toward Shiyu. “Potential new hire. Used to work the other club. Got shut down. He’s mad I have his old regulars. I told him to apply instead of swinging.”
Tatsuya’s brow rose, amused. Janice studied Shiyu for a second, then the calm way Curtis had handled the floor. “We are hiring. Floor interview starts with not assaulting my staff. You want to keep talking, you do it in my office after you finish that drink. Or security walks you out. Your choice.”
Shiyu looked between them, then at the woman who had already asked for him, and slowly picked up the glass. The crowd had gone back to their own tables. Curtis stayed seated, expression even, while the low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet and the unexpected meeting continued under the club lights.
Shiyu took the drink. The anger had cooled into something wary and tired. He glanced at the woman who had recognized him—she was still nearby, pretending to check her phone—then back at Janice.
“Office is fine,” he said. “I don’t want security. I just… didn’t expect the regulars to move this fast.”
Janice nodded once. “Finish the glass. Then we talk. Tatsuya will walk you back so no one else decides to join the meeting.”
Tatsuya gave Curtis a small, approving look and gestured for Shiyu to stand. The man did, slower this time. As they headed toward the hallway the woman drifted closer again, hopeful. Curtis caught her eye and shook his head once, polite.
“Later,” he said. “If he sticks around.”
She smiled, flushed, and let them pass. The floor settled. A few patrons were still murmuring about the “dance,” but the next act had the mic and the moment was already fading into the night’s ordinary noise.
Hina appeared at Curtis’s shoulder with a fresh tray. “You turned a fight into a job interview.”
“Seemed better than breaking his wrists in front of customers.” He stood, straightening his tie. “Janice will sort it. If he’s any good she’ll keep him. If not, he leaves quieter than he came in.”
She bumped his arm, warm. “You’re ridiculous. Also, table four is asking if you take requests now. I told them maybe after your section’s covered.”
“Tell them I’ll think about it.” He picked up the half-finished glasses and headed for the bar. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the club kept moving, Shiyu disappeared into the office with Tatsuya, and open mic night continued like the angry entrance had been just another part of the show.
Tatsuya lingered after Shiyu was settled in the office. He found Curtis at the bar restocking and leaned on the counter, voice low.
“You could’ve dropped him faster. Why the dance?”
Curtis set a glass in the well. “This isn’t U.A. I’m not here as the Crypt King.”
Tatsuya’s brow rose. “Crypt King?”
“My hero name.” Curtis kept his tone even. “Star and Stripe gave it to me. Since I grew up in Appalachia around cryptids, fae, and other forest things.” He met Tatsuya’s eyes. “It would be bad for our image if a host starts beating people down in our club. I care about you and Janice. If I jeopardized this place I’d find it hard to move past that.”
Tatsuya was quiet for a second. The floor noise covered them. He nodded, slow. “That’s why Janice keeps you. Most guys your age would’ve made the scene about themselves.” He straightened. “Crypt King stays outside. In here you’re Beardy. We clear?”
“Clear.”
Tatsuya clapped him once on the shoulder and headed back toward the office. Curtis finished the restock. Hina glanced over from her section, caught the tail end of the calm, and didn’t ask. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the club continued and the name he used under different lights remained exactly where he’d left it.
The club had quieted. Last customers were gone. Lights were down to closing levels. Staff moved through the usual shutdown—chairs up, glasses racked, the open-mic board wiped clean. Curtis was wiping his section when Shiyu approached, slower than he had on the way in. The office talk had clearly happened. He stopped a polite distance away.
“Hey, um. Curtis, was it? Look, I’m really sorry. And thank you.” He bowed, short and stiff. “Janice said I can start on glass and support next week if I want it. I do.”
Curtis tilted his head. “It’s fine. You don’t seem bad. Just upset.”
He didn’t mention the check. Jeeves had run it while Shiyu was in the office—clean record, taxes paid, no complaints from former coworkers, a couple of volunteer shifts at a local shelter on his days off. Upstanding enough. The anger had been about the lost clients, not a pattern. Curtis had already decided that before the man walked back out.
Shiyu straightened, some of the tension leaving his shoulders. “Still. Swinging at you was stupid. You could’ve made it worse. You didn’t.”
“Would’ve been worse for the club.” Curtis set the rag down. “Show up on time. Treat the floor like it’s yours to protect. You’ll be fine.”
Hina passed with the last of the flyer bags, caught the bow, and smiled small at Curtis before continuing. Janice called from the bar that the safe was ready whenever they were done. Tatsuya was already locking the front. Shiyu nodded once more and headed for the side door to wait for his ride.
Curtis finished the table. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the club shut down around him and the man who had come in swinging left with a shift instead of a bruise.
Hina waited by the side door while Curtis clocked out. Jacket on, bag over her shoulder, the night air cool when they stepped out together. The club lights clicked off behind them. They fell into step toward the station without discussing it.
“Shiyu looked lighter when he left,” she said after a block. “Janice must’ve been decent to him.”
“She was.” Curtis’s hands stayed in his pockets. “He’ll start on support. If he keeps his head he’ll be fine.”
Hina bumped his arm, warm. “You turned a swing into a hire. Again. You’re getting good at that.”
“Rather have another set of hands than a scene.” He glanced at her. “You tired?”
“A little. Open mic nights run longer.” She smiled, small. “Worth it. They liked the song. A couple of my tables asked if you do requests.”
“Maybe next time.” The corner of his mouth twitched. “If the face stays clean.”
They walked under the streetlights. Their shoulders brushed every few steps. The usual flush sat on her cheeks, quiet and familiar. Neither of them hurried. The train wasn’t for another ten minutes. The low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet while the city moved around them and the walk home stayed exactly as simple as it needed to be.
Hina glanced at him as they reached the platform, then winked. “Now let’s hurry home, so you can make Yui and me sing.”
Curtis blinked. “You guys sing?”
She pinched the bridge of her nose and chuckled, cheeks pink. “I’ll tell you when we get home.”
“Alright.” He didn’t push. The train arrived. They stepped on, standing close as the doors shut. Hina’s shoulder stayed against his arm the whole ride. The low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet while the city lights streaked past the windows and the explanation waited for the apartment upstairs.
